{% extends "voc2_en.j2" %}

{% block summary %}
<p>OSLO-Person focuses on describing a natural person, i.e., a physical person and not an organization. Natural persons have their own legal personality, which is not artificial as with legal entities.</p><p>The vocabulary is an extension of existing RDF vocabularies about people. These are solely intended to identify a person (e.g., the <a class="c2" href="https://joinup.ec.europa.eu/node/42440">ISA Core Person Vocabulary</a> , which models name, gender, date of birth, among other things) or are designed for specific applications (e.g., <a class="c2" href="http://xmlns.com/foaf/spec/">FOAF</a> , which mainly focuses on relationships between people). </p><p>What was still missing is a vocabulary for the type of information typically kept in a national population register. This concerns data with civil law implications such as descent, birth, marriage. The inclusion of such data in a register confirms its official status and guarantees the person concerned the rights and obligations attached to it. </p><p>The OSLO Person vocabulary is more extensive than, for example, ISA Core Person, but is not modeled on a specific national register. An attempt has been made to select the civil law characteristics that appear in all national population registers. To still describe the details of a specific population register, the user must expand the data model. </p><p>Compared to ISA Core Person, personal relationships of various kinds are added: descent, marriage, cohabitation, guardianship, and family relationships. The entity Family has been added, as well as Civil Status and Nationality. Also new is the elaboration of the existing relationship in ISA Core Person between a person and a jurisdiction. This concerns the relationships citizenship and residency, whereby in OSLO-Person and respectively a distinction can be made between citizens and foreigners, and between residents and non-residents. The place of residence of a person and their more specialized form Domicile (with corresponding official address) can now also be exchanged. The address provided by ISA is part of the contact info. </p><p>Data on property rights, criminal law, or social law data fall outside the OSLO Person vocabulary.</p>	
{% endblock %}

